---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/replenishment';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is a reorder estimate a depletion date?",
    "answer": "No. Purchase history and quantity provide a hypothesis, not a measurement of household supply."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should paused subscribers receive manual reorder offers?",
    "answer": "Do not assume that. A pause can mean excess supply; use the actual state and customer preference."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should happen after a new purchase?",
    "answer": "Cancel the obsolete same-product reminder and calculate eligibility from the new order."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does every reminder need a discount?",
    "answer": "No. Evaluate convenience first and treat any incentive as a separate decision with measurable costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Shopify replenishment email: useful reorder timing</h1>
    <p>Help customers reorder consumables without pretending to know what remains in their cupboard. Start from product quantities and purchase history, then make the reminder easy to pause or correct.</p>
    <section class="py-8">
      <h2>Define the product and quantity model</h2>
      <p>Group only products that share a meaningful consumption pattern. Variant size, quantity purchased and bundles can change the interval. A repeat order may reflect a gift, stock-up purchase or promotion rather than an empty supply. Do not describe an estimated reorder date as a measured depletion date.</p>
      <p>Separate trial packs from standard sizes and consumables from durable accessories. Start with a documented timing hypothesis for each eligible group. Klaviyo’s replenishment guidance recommends reviewing purchase data to identify buying cycles; that supports a data-informed delay, not a universal reminder schedule or proof of consumption.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="py-8">
      <h2>Use intervals with their limitations</h2>
      <p>Measure time between comparable purchases, excluding refunds and unrelated items. Record whether the interval begins at purchase, dispatch or delivery. Customers who never reorder do not have an observed repeat interval; leaving them out makes a repeat-buyer median an incomplete view of the whole audience.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical coffee order containing 500 grams at an assumed 25 grams per day, estimated supply is 20 days. This arithmetic is an illustration, not a usage recommendation or customer observation. Delivery lag, shared use and existing supply can all shift the useful reminder date. Offer “remind me later” rather than asserting “you have run out.”</p>
    </section>
    <section class="py-8">
      <h2>Recheck subscriptions and new purchases</h2>
      <p>Before sending, check whether an active subscription already covers the product and quantity. Paused, skipped and cancelled subscriptions are different states: a pause may mean excess supply, not a request for a manual purchase offer. If subscription state is stale or unavailable, avoid a definitive claim about the next shipment.</p>
      <p>A new purchase of the same product should cancel the obsolete reminder and establish a new basis for timing. Returns, unresolved quality concerns and replacement orders need explicit handling. For mixed-product orders, maintain separate eligibility records while consolidating messages when reminders would otherwise crowd the inbox.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="py-8">
      <h2>Make the reorder destination useful</h2>
      <p>Link to the correct size and variant and show current availability on the page. If a seasonal tea is gone, explain the change; do not silently substitute a different product into a one-click reorder promise. Validate any prepared cart before implying the buyer can repeat the exact order.</p>
      <p>Start with convenience and relevant product information. An incentive is a separate commercial decision with actual costs and terms, not an automatic requirement for every reminder. A subscription invitation should explain the real schedule and controls; repeated reminders should stop when the buyer declines or postpones them.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="py-8">
      <h2>A proposed replenishment evaluation</h2>
      <p>For a tea catalog, model a small tin, a larger tin and a mixed basket. Add a same-item repurchase before the scheduled reminder, a paused subscription and an unavailable variant. Confirm that each state changes the queued message appropriately. These are proposed acceptance checks, not merchant results.</p>
      <p>Ask Sequenzy, Klaviyo or another candidate to show that event history using your available data and actual subscription integration. Compare reminder eligibility, exits and sync visibility before comparing templates. Measure repeat purchase over a suitable product cycle, margin after incentives, postponements and complaints. A holdout for optional reminders can help distinguish additional orders from purchases that merely occurred earlier.</p>
    </section>
    <p>Primary reference checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360003195232">Klaviyo replenishment flow guidance</a>. Workflow recommendations and proposed checks above are editorial guidance, not claims of completed testing.</p>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post purchase</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">winback</a>. Browse the <a class="link" href="/apps">tool catalog</a> for candidates; confirm current capabilities and costs for the required integration.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Replenishment FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
